## Contributing to mochiOS Libraries ##
Thank you for your interest in contributing to mochiOS Libraries!

This repository maintains the infrastructure for building and distributing
third-party libraries for mochiOS.

Contributions are welcome, including new library ports, improvements to
existing recipes, bug fixes, and build infrastructure improvements.



Before contributing
-----------------------------------------
Before starting work, please check existing issues and pull requests to
avoid duplicate efforts.

For new library ports, we recommend opening a library request issue before
beginning implementation.



Requesting a library
-----------------------------------------
To request support for a new library, open an issue using the library
request template.

Please provide:

  - Library name and upstream repository
  - Brief description and intended use
  - License information
  - Required dependencies
  - Known compatibility or porting considerations

A request does not guarantee that a library will be added.



Adding a library
-----------------------------------------
New libraries are registered in libraries.toml.

To add a library:

  1. Add the library definition to libraries.toml.
  2. Declare its dependencies.
  3. Create a build recipe under recipes/<library-name>/.
  4. Add the required build configuration and verification probe.
  5. Ensure the upstream license is correctly identified.
  6. Run the available validation and tests.

Any required upstream patches should be minimal and limited to the
relevant library.



Modifying existing libraries
-----------------------------------------
Changes to existing libraries should preserve compatibility whenever
possible.

When updating a library:

  - Verify upstream source and version rules.
  - Review dependency changes.
  - Check for ABI compatibility concerns.
  - Update build recipes and verification probes as necessary.

Avoid unrelated modifications.



Validation and testing
-----------------------------------------
Before submitting a pull request, run:

  python3 tools/libs.py check
  python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v

All applicable checks should pass.

If testing cannot be completed, explain the reason in the pull request.

Do not claim that a library has been tested on mochiOS unless such testing
has actually been performed.



Pull requests
-----------------------------------------
Submit contributions through pull requests against the main branch.

Each pull request should:

  - Clearly describe the changes and their purpose.
  - Reference related issues when applicable.
  - Include relevant testing results.
  - Identify any limitations or known issues.
  - Avoid unrelated modifications.

Maintainers may request changes before merging.



Licensing
-----------------------------------------
Third-party libraries retain their original upstream licenses.

Contributors must ensure that required copyright notices and license
information are preserved.

Contributions must comply with applicable licenses and redistribution
requirements.



Reporting issues
-----------------------------------------

Bug reports and improvement suggestions are welcome.

Please include the affected library, relevant configuration, error messages,
and steps to reproduce the problem when applicable.

Cross-build backends
-----------------------------------------
Recipes select cmake, meson, or autotools. All backends use the SDK's
mochiOS compiler drivers and are followed by ELF and consumer-link checks.
Target executables are never run during the build. Meson uses a cross file
with needs_exe_wrapper=true and disables subproject downloads. Its install_tags
must select library/development data rather than target command-line tools.

Autotools recipes use explicit --host and --build triples. Optional bootstrap
is limited to autoreconf. configure contains argument arrays, not shell text.
Build/install subdirectories and generated public headers can be declared
when an upstream package also contains unrelated executables.

patches lists repository-owned unified patches relative to the recipe. They
are applied after bootstrap to a fresh disposable source copy. Their hashes
are part of the frozen build identity and verified package provenance. A
changed upstream context fails the build instead of silently skipping a patch.

Source submodules must declare HTTPS URLs and full commits in libraries.toml.
Every gitlink and .gitmodules URL is checked; unknown/nested dependencies and
modified cached sources are rejected. The network is unavailable during the
actual library build. Dependency prefixes include the transitive closure, so
pkg-config cannot fall back to host libraries for indirect dependencies.

Official generated source distributions can declare source_archive as an HTTPS
URL template containing {version}. sources.lock.json records the corresponding
Git tag/commit and archive URL/SHA-256. Refreshing to a newer upstream release
freezes its distribution digest before building; the bytes of an already pinned
release must never change. Cached extracted sources have a complete inventory
and are rejected if modified. No build commands are run while fetching sources.

Known platform requirements
-----------------------------------------
GLib is enabled with the published SDK 27.0-dp.1-shared.3. Its resolver
interfaces cover the record types required by GIO. The native GIO TLS port,
libsoup and WebKit remain disabled until their separate SDK/runtime and
platform-integration requirements are satisfied.

See tooling/platform-requirements.json for each port's verification evidence
and limitations. mochiOS uses startup-linked shared libraries; successful
cross-compilation does not establish runtime loading or native OS behavior.
Libgcrypt uses the SDK getentropy interface and native libgpg-error locks.
The pinned SDK does not provide mlock, so OS-backed locked memory is not
available in this port. Linux reference tests do not verify mochiOS runtime.

Manual library releases
-----------------------------------------
Library releases belong to mochiOS/libs, separately from the mochiOS SDK.
Each upstream library version has its own release tag, such as
libgcrypt-1.12.4. Build revisions are part of asset filenames, not that tag.
These are development prereleases verified by cross-compilation and linking;
only claim native runtime testing when it has actually been performed.

1. Commit the reviewed recipes and source locks, run the checks below, and
   push the intended commit yourself. Keep sdk.lock.toml pinned to a published
   SDK with its reviewed archive hash and builder image digest.

     python3 tools/libs.py check
     python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v
     git push origin main

2. In GitHub Actions for mochiOS/libs, manually run
   "Track, build and release libraries" on main with refresh=false.
   The current workflow builds and verifies artifacts; it does not publish
   GitHub releases. Leave refresh disabled to use the reviewed source lock.
   A scheduled run may select newer tags, so use a successful manual run for
   this release. Every build uses the pinned isolated container; the existing
   mochiOS workspace and host libraries are not mounted into it.

3. From that same successful run, download both frozen-plan and
   verified-libraries. Keep a clean checkout of the workflow's exact commit
   when verifying them locally. Python 3.12+ and readelf are required.
   For example, from the repository root:

     gh run download RUN_ID --repo mochiOS/libs --name frozen-plan --dir out/release-plan
     gh run download RUN_ID --repo mochiOS/libs --name verified-libraries --dir out/release-assets
     python3 tools/releases.py verify --plan out/release-plan/plan.json --artifacts out/release-assets

   Expect one directory per release under out/release-assets. For every
   package, upload all three files with the same stem:

     <library>-<version>-r<revision>-x86_64-unknown-mochios-mochios-1.tar.gz
     <library>-<version>-r<revision>-x86_64-unknown-mochios-mochios-1.json
     <library>-<version>-r<revision>-x86_64-unknown-mochios-mochios-1.sha256

   The archive contains the target sysroot and an embedded manifest; the
   external JSON records source, SDK, recipe, verification and file hashes.
   The .sha256 file covers the archive and JSON and acts as a completion
   marker. Download the outer Actions ZIP and extract it; do not upload the
   Actions ZIP or rename the three library files.

4. On the mochiOS/libs Releases page, create a release for each new library
   tag using the workflow's exact commit. Mark it as a prerelease, finish
   uploading all three files, review the notes, then publish manually.
   For an existing tag, add the new revision's archive and JSON first and its
   .sha256 file last. Preserve all older revisions and never overwrite an
   existing asset with different bytes. If an existing revision's inputs or
   bytes changed, increment revision in libraries.toml and rebuild it and
   affected dependents before publication.

The scripts stop on failed builds, failed import checks or changed immutable
release inputs. Do not bypass those checks or turn locally compiled test
prefixes into release packages: they have not passed the isolated production
pipeline and its provenance/path controls. Local out/text-port files are
port-development evidence, not release assets.

The tools/releases.py publish subcommand publishes the complete selected
plan. It is intentionally not used in the manual steps above.
